 @marcel-q1m  The original playback was very likely from contention for resources on the GPU, as it would be most pronounced, as in the video would stop but audio would keep going and then the two would be out of sync from then until the video was restarted, when a game was particularly hard on the video card, such as when a round of World of Warships would just be getting started.  This was also only a problem with AMD cards, as my old RTX2080 would not have this problem.  The CPU being taxed would cause a similar problem, but was much less common.  When I put in the secondary card and was able to assign certain tasks to it through the Windows Hybrid video functions, the problem went away.  (Windows 11 made this WAY easier.)

The problems I'm having now are different from back before this started.  Today, I'm getting more of a low frame rate stuttering problem only during certain times.  I have a third monitor that I use for diagnostic info.  I usually keep it turned off, but use it for troubleshooting, just a 10" 1024X600 thing, to show HWINFO and Task Manager.  I can see that during this stuttering, neither GPU is being heavily utilized, and it happens whether streaming video is running or not.  So, I know resource contention is not an issue.  It is most likely something in the drivers, but I'm not sure what.  

As for using the same brand video card, I do have a 6600XT I can put in, but it would require dropping my 10Gb NIC, as the 6600XT runs into the connectors along the bottom of the board, including the from panel connectors. I'd have to go down to using the 2.5Gb onboard NIC, which would be annoying to me.

